## Proof

**Claim:** The maximum number of UFOs that can land on a single planet is **5**.

---

### Upper Bound: At most 5

Suppose planets $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_k$ all send their UFOs to planet $O$. Then for each $A_i$, planet $O$ is its nearest neighbor, so:

$$|A_iO| < |A_iA_j| \quad \text{for all } j \neq i.$$

Consider any pair $A_i, A_j$. We have both $|A_iO| < |A_iA_j|$ and $|A_jO| < |A_iA_j|$. Thus in triangle $A_i O A_j$, the side $A_iA_j$ is **strictly** the longest side. The angle opposite the longest side is the largest angle in the triangle. Since the triangle is not equilateral (the longest side is strictly longest), this largest angle $\angle A_i O A_j$ is **strictly greater than 60°**.

Now arrange $A_1, \ldots, A_k$ in angular order around $O$, and let $\theta_1, \theta_2, \ldots, \theta_k$ be the consecutive angles between adjacent planets. Each consecutive pair is also a pair sending to $O$, so each $\theta_i > 60°$. Since the angles sum to $360°$:

$$k \cdot 60° < \theta_1 + \theta_2 + \cdots + \theta_k = 360°$$

This gives $k < 6$, hence $k \leq 5$.

---

### Lower Bound: 5 is achievable

**Construction.** Place planet $O$ at the origin. Place five planets $A_1, \ldots, A_5$ at distance $1$ from $O$ at angles $0°, 72°, 144°, 216°, 288°$ respectively. The distance between any two adjacent planets (72° apart) is $2\sin 36° \approx 1.176 > 1$, and non-adjacent pairs are even farther. So $O$ is the nearest neighbor of each $A_i$.

Now apply a small generic perturbation: set $|A_iO| = r_i$ with distinct $r_i$ near $1$, and angles $\alpha_i$ near $72° \cdot i$ with all consecutive gaps still exceeding $60°$ (possible since $72° > 60°$ gives slack). Choose the perturbation generically so that **all pairwise distances among all planets are distinct** — the set of configurations where any two distances coincide has measure zero, so a generic choice avoids all coincidences. The perturbation is small enough that $O$ remains the nearest neighbor of each $A_i$.

Place all remaining (infinitely many) planets at distances greater than $10$ from $O$ and from each other, with all pairwise distances distinct and distinct from all existing distances. This ensures no additional planet has $O$ as its nearest neighbor, and the global uniqueness of distances is maintained.

Thus five planets $A_1, \ldots, A_5$ all send their UFOs to $O$, achieving $5$.

---

### Conclusion

The maximum number of UFOs that can land on a single planet is $\boxed{5}$.

